Figure 1: Photometric and kinematic properties of NGC 5717.
From left to right and top to bottom: (i) a composite RGB image from the Legacy Imaging Surveys (Dey et al., 2019); the yellow and gray hexagons indicate the MaNGA fields of view, and the red ellipses trace the visual boundary of the embedded CR disk, whose semi-major axis is 11 arcsec, or 8 kpc;
(ii) an unsharp-masked image based on the CFHT $r$-band data, highlighting the three-ring structure of the disk;
(iii–vi) maps derived from the one-component NBursts analysis (Sect. 3.1): H$\alpha$ flux $F_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}$, stellar line-of-sight velocity $V_{\star}$, stellar velocity dispersion $\sigma_{\star}$, and ionized-gas velocity $V_{\mathrm{gas}}$.
For the $F_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}$ and $V_{\mathrm{gas}}$ maps, only spaxels with a signal-to-noise ratio greater than 5 in the H$\alpha$ emission line are shown.

Figure 10: DIB $\lambda 6614$ parameters measured with MCMC-RVSD ($x$-axis) versus template matching ($y$-axis) for the three degeneracy classes. Error bars are the $1\sigma$ uncertainties of both methods and the dashed line is the 1:1 relation. In each panel the upper row gives the mean offset $\bar{\Delta}=\langle\mathrm{template}-\mathrm{MCMC}\rangle$ and the lower row the intrinsic correlation coefficient $\rho$ (errors-in-variables maximum-likelihood estimate, per-source errors on both axes). “N/A” marks cases where the quoted uncertainties account for the
entire scatter, for which $\rho$ is not identifiable. Circles: Success;
triangles: Mild Degeneracy; crosses: Severe Degeneracy.
